--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  V0 = ruby 2.1.3  (REXML 3.1.7.3, pre CVE-2014-8080; fully vulnerable)
  V1 = ruby 2.1.4  (REXML 3.1.7.3, CVE-2014-8080 patched = TARGET)   <- incomplete
  V2 = ruby 2.1.5  (REXML 3.1.7.3, CVE-2014-8090 patched = complete)

  In-image source check (grep on /usr/local/lib/ruby/2.1.0/rexml/*.rb):
    ruby:2.1.3  entity.rb: NO 'grown too large'   document.rb: NO 'def document'
    ruby:2.1.4  entity.rb: 145 raise "entity expansion has grown too large"  document.rb: NO 'def document'
    ruby:2.1.5  entity.rb: 145 raise "entity expansion has grown too large"  document.rb: 281 def document
  => the byte-cap (CVE-2014-8080) appears at 2.1.4; the Document#document fix
     (CVE-2014-8090) appears only at 2.1.5. Exactly the documented incomplete-fix pair,
     in the REAL bundled library — nothing lifted or hand-edited.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BASE IMAGE ACQUISITION (the buildability wrinkle, solved)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modern Docker (server 29.4.0) REFUSES to pull the 2016-era official ruby:2.1.x images:
    Error response from daemon: unsupported manifest media type and no default
    available: application/vnd.docker.distribution.manifest.v1+prettyjws
(these images predate schema-v2 manifests). Resolved WITHOUT lifting REXML by pulling the
REAL official images with skopeo (which supports schema-v1) and converting into the daemon:
    skopeo copy --override-os linux --override-arch amd64 \
        docker://docker.io/library/ruby:<ver> docker-daemon:ruby:<ver>-official
Loaded: ruby:2.1.3-official (804MB), ruby:2.1.4-official (798MB), ruby:2.1.5-official (777MB).
Docker build uses the LEGACY builder (DOCKER_BUILDKIT=0 COMPOSE_DOCKER_CLI_BUILD=0) so the
daemon-loaded bases resolve (BuildKit only looks at registries, not the daemon image store).
These are amd64 images run under emulation on arm64 macOS; REXML/WEBrick are pure Ruby so
behavior is faithful.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
REAL RUBY WEB APP (app/server.rb, WEBrick stdlib)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  GET  /health -> {"ok":true,"ruby":"<ver>","rexml":"3.1.7.3"}
  POST /parse   body = raw XML -> REXML::Document.new(body) + each_recursive text expansion,
                run SYNCHRONOUSLY in the request handler, then JSON {status,server_ms,...}.
  REXML kept at stock 2014 defaults (entity_expansion_limit=10000, text_limit=10240).
  Health probe against V1:  {"ok":true,"ruby":"2.1.4","rexml":"3.1.7.3"}   (real HTTP)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PAYLOADS  (payloads/*.xml — all < 3 KB on disk; the DoS is in expansion WORK, not size)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
benign.xml            one trivial entity; parses instantly on all versions.
original_attack.xml   (CVE-2014-8080) nested PARAMETER entities a..j (10x/level), bomb=%j;*10,
                      referenced by <root>&bomb;</root>. %pe; value expansion goes through
                      Entity#value: UNCAPPED on V0 (exponential text -> DoS); the CVE-2014-8080
                      byte cap raises "entity expansion has grown too large" on V1/V2.
residual_attack.xml   (CVE-2014-8090) EMPTY nested parameter entities a0..a30 (a1="%a0;%a0;" ...
                      a30="%a29;%a29;"), then <!ENTITY % trigger "%a30;"> %trigger; referenced
                      INSIDE the internal DTD subset. Each expansion yields "" so the per-value
                      byte cap is NEVER tripped (sum stays 0), yet the COUNT of expansions is
                      2**30. During DTD parse the Document has no root -> document==nil on V0/V1
                      -> record_entity_expansion (count limit) never called -> unbounded CPU ->
                      DoS. On V2, Document#document=>self makes the count limit fire at 10000.
                      Pure-CPU, flat memory (host-safe).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MEASURED 3x3 MATRIX  (real Docker; EXTERNAL HTTP POST /parse; cpus=1.0; mem_limit 1200m;
client read timeout 10s; fresh server per probe). status from the app's JSON verdict.
DoS iff hang(timeout)/crash/latency>2000ms; BOUNDED iff <500ms & status in {parsed,limited}.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  payload   |  V0 (2.1.3)          |  V1 (2.1.4, TARGET)   |  V2 (2.1.5)
  ----------+----------------------+-----------------------+----------------------
  benign    |  parsed   11.8 ms    |  parsed   9.8 ms      |  parsed   10.0 ms
  original  |  timeout  10001 ms   |  limited  14.0 ms     |  limited  13.8 ms
  residual  |  timeout  10001 ms   |  timeout  10001 ms    |  limited  58.4 ms

  V1 original raise (observed over HTTP):
     RuntimeError "entity expansion has grown too large"
     /usr/local/lib/ruby/2.1.0/rexml/entity.rb:145:in `block in value'   (CVE-2014-8080 text cap)
  V2 residual raise (observed over HTTP):
     RuntimeError "number of entity expansions exceeded, processing aborted."
     /usr/local/lib/ruby/2.1.0/rexml/document.rb:277:in `record_entity_expansion'  (CVE-2014-8090 count limit)
  -> the residual is caught by a DIFFERENT guard at a DIFFERENT file/line, at a DIFFERENT
     fixed version, than the original: a genuine incomplete-fix residual, on real bundled REXML.

Separation is unambiguous: residual on V1 = 10001 ms (hard HTTP timeout, server hung) vs
benign 9.8 ms => >1000x, and V1 raises NO limit at all (unbounded), whereas V2 raises the
count-limit error in ~58 ms.

